WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.838 --> 00:00:08.520
[PIANO MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.300
PROFESSOR: I'm very pleased that John White has agreed to talk with me today

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.370
about our accountability system in the United States and the various efforts

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:24.270
to raise standards through Common Core and through many other actions taken

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:25.680
at state levels.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.270
John White is the state superintendent for education

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:34.980
for the state of Louisiana and was also deputy chancellor in New York City.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:40.800
So he has a breadth of knowledge about how the inter-governmental system needs

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.030
to work.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:46.020
And it's very especially interesting to talk

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:51.510
with somebody who has been so successful in Louisiana at raising student

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:56.970
performance from what was second to the bottom to the most

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:01.870
rapidly improving state in the United States under his leadership.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.952
So John, it's great to have you with me today.

00:01:03.952 --> 00:01:05.160
JOHN WHITE: Great to be here.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.340
PROFESSOR: And I would like to ask you, is testing

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.740
an important part of your thinking?

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.170
Is holding schools accountable an important part

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:18.870
of your thinking about how you get gains in student performance and a more

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:20.910
effective educational system?

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:21.660
JOHN WHITE: It is.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.980
And no question that measurement has to be an important component

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.394
of any plan for success.

00:01:27.394 --> 00:01:30.060
There's a lot of ways that we can improve testing, a lot of ways

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.230
that we can improve our accountability systems.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.380
Every Student Succeeds Act, I think, at least

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.860
calls us to can seriously consider those questions in the States.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.770
There are some serious risks involved in all of this, too.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:42.285
But no question, if you--

00:01:42.285 --> 00:01:45.570
the step to begin to measure learning is an important one.

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:46.800
It can be done better.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.480
It can be done more efficiently.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.960
But it's a critical component as a starting point of any plan for change.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.780
PROFESSOR: Well, just a lot of people were on board with this idea

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:59.160
back in 2002 when No Child Left Behind was introduced into Congress

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.380
in a bipartisan coalition.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:06.190
Democrats and Republicans alike supported this by overwhelming margin.

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:09.449
But as time went on, a lot of opposition developed

00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:12.840
to the testing and the holding schools accountable that

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.810
was required by No Child Left Behind.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:20.587
What did they do wrong or why did that political opposition develop?

00:02:20.587 --> 00:02:23.670
JOHN WHITE: Well, I think there's a difference between what was done wrong

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.830
and why the political opposition developed.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.350
But I think that in terms of what was done wrong,

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:31.200
the president has talked about this.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.550
It has spawned an industry.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.280
No question about that.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:37.870
And I'm not sure if every element of that industry is what we want.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:40.600
We don't need our kids taking tests every day,

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.450
multiple times over the course of a day.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:43.590
It should be a checkup.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:44.910
And that's what it is.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:48.720
And it's fair to say that we or the establishment or industry

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.040
have not effectively communicated to parents, this is how this benefits you.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.880
This is not just a crusade to benefit the disenfranchised.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:57.900
This is for everybody.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:58.810
This is beneficial.

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:01.869
And we haven't done as much as we needed to communicate that.

00:03:01.869 --> 00:03:03.660
PROFESSOR: Well, one of the mistakes that I

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.950
thought was made by No Child Left Behind was the adequate yearly progress

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:12.780
way of defining what was a good school, instead of looking at how much kids are

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:16.140
learning in the course of one year, how much more do they know

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:19.530
this year than they did one year ago, which I would have thought

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.620
would have been the logical way of trying to find out

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.489
if a school was effective or not.

00:03:24.489 --> 00:03:26.280
JOHN WHITE: The good news is people I think

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:29.190
discovered that quickly, years into No Child Left Behind,

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:32.490
that the idea of just how many students are

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:36.420
at a certain level in a certain school is not really an effective way

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.580
of measuring the effect of that school.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:42.960
In our state for example, if you look at the A schools or the schools that

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.610
are rated A, 30% of, in some schools 90% of struggling students,

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.500
in those schools are hitting the mark.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.361
In some state, in some of those a-rated schools, only 30%

00:03:52.361 --> 00:03:54.360
of the struggling students are hitting the mark.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:57.300
So you've got wide, wide differences when you really

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.180
dig into the data behind what is an A school.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:03.240
PROFESSOR: So you will give an A to a school

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:07.050
where the students are performing at a fairly low level, if they're

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:10.407
showing substantial improvement from one year to the next.

00:04:10.407 --> 00:04:12.240
JOHN WHITE: Well, I think we should do that.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:15.439
I think right now what we do, and I probably didn't explain it

00:04:15.439 --> 00:04:16.480
as well as I should have.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:19.500
But what we do is we give A's to schools that

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.120
are making great strides with kids.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.630
We also give A's to some schools that aren't making great strides,

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.160
but just have high performing kids to begin with.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:29.500
And there's obviously a distinction there.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:33.130
An A school can be one where kids are just performing at a high level.

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:37.800
But it can also be one where kids came performing at a deplorably low level,

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:39.030
and then really made gains.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:40.110
That's an A also.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:43.170
And we need to redefine our state, and in many states,

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:45.910
what constitutes an A school.

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:50.580
PROFESSOR: Now the state school chief officers, I've

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.250
forgotten exactly how that name goes.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:56.490
And the governors, the National Governors Association,

00:04:56.490 --> 00:05:00.450
agreed to put into place Common Core State Standards.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:02.790
That was backed by the Obama administration,

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:06.280
with its race to the top initiative and its waiver policies,

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:11.610
so that states didn't have to abide by some of those NCLB requirements,

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:16.410
if, in fact, they would adopt the Common Core State Standards

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:19.780
and make other reforms, as well.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:24.580
So once again, this at the beginning had widespread support.

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:28.950
And well, let's first of all talk about that widespread support.

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:31.392
Do you agree that it was there at the beginning?

00:05:31.392 --> 00:05:32.100
JOHN WHITE: I do.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:36.210
And you know, the most simple way that I can rationalize it in my mind

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.940
and for those that I work with is, we have historically

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:42.450
been the 48 or 49th rank state in the country in academics.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:45.327
We have historically been in the top five in football.

00:05:45.327 --> 00:05:47.160
The reason we're in the top five in football

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:50.740
is because we're willing to go to any state, and we're willing to compete.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:54.570
But in academics, it's always just been, well, how did one town in Louisiana

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:56.400
do against another town?

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.170
We can't get to the top in a valid way unless we're willing to go to any state

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:03.150
and put our education system up against it.

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:04.720
That's how you grow.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:07.800
That's how you progress is you're willing to open your doors

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:08.820
and to compete.

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.670
So the principles of success on the football field

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:14.410
are principles of success in our academic system.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:17.490
If we don't have something that we share with other states, the ability

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:19.770
to measure ourselves against everybody else,

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:24.040
there's not really a valid platform for how we need to progress.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:27.150
PROFESSOR: But a lot of people feel like, OK, the history of education

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:31.560
in the South, in general, Louisiana included, is very different

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:34.770
than the history of education in the Northeast, where you've had,

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:37.050
back to the days of the pilgrims, a real commitment

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:41.310
to teaching children early on.

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:43.740
Is it fair to put them on the same platform

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:48.540
and say, well, you've got to be compared to those people

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:51.450
in other parts of the country, not just your own state?

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:52.290
JOHN WHITE: Well, you know, there's a difference

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:55.040
between what's fair to the adults and what's fair to the students.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:56.820
And you need to be fair to both.

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:02.070
We have 300 years of subjugation of African-American people in our state.

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:06.150
We are an agrarian state that is coming into a 21st century economy

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:07.412
and natural resources state.

00:07:07.412 --> 00:07:09.870
I'm not going to argue that that's a different, that that's

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:13.180
the same conditions in which Massachusetts or New York,

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:15.120
or a state in the Northeast operates in.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.510
And clearly we come into this from a different perspective.

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:21.870
But what is fair to the kids who are inheriting in the year

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:28.410
2016, a multinational, seriously competitive economy,

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:32.250
is to have expectations that are those that the workforce will

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:35.280
have for them, which is you should be able to work and live and succeed

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:36.270
anywhere.

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:39.164
What's fair to the adults is not to demoralize everybody

00:07:39.164 --> 00:07:41.080
just because you have those high expectations.

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:42.980
And you can do both of those things.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:44.730
You can have a system that has the highest

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:47.340
expectations possible for children, while having

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:51.030
a rational system of improvement for the adults who operate the system.

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:56.280
PROFESSOR: So the Common Core standards principle is one that you endorse,

00:07:56.280 --> 00:08:01.980
you continue to endorse, despite the political opposition

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:06.260
that developed on both the right and the left of the political spectrum.

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:09.690
The Tea Party opposed it, a lot of teacher unions opposed it,

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:13.050
a lot of teachers have opposed Common Core.

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:15.750
So why-- I want to ask, especially about the teachers.

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:21.542
Why did opposition to Common Core State Standards develop among teachers?

00:08:21.542 --> 00:08:23.250
JOHN WHITE: Well, I support the principle

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:26.220
of what those standards embody which is interstate competition.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:29.310
You know, we have our own standards in Louisiana called, the Louisiana

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:31.649
students standards that allow us to remain comparable

00:08:31.649 --> 00:08:32.940
with states across the country.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:35.010
And I support those standards.

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:37.740
With respect to teachers first, there is no one teacher voice.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:40.679
And I am sick and tired of people saying that teachers view this,

00:08:40.679 --> 00:08:42.309
or teachers believe it that way.

00:08:42.309 --> 00:08:44.267
You know, every year I get in a room with 5,000

00:08:44.267 --> 00:08:47.340
or 6,000 teachers in New Orleans for a group, a gathering called,

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:48.600
Our Teacher Leaders.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:50.790
There are 5,000 or 6,000 different views about a lot

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:52.050
of different stuff in there.

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:55.410
But there is no one in that room who's just against progress.

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:58.962
And I actually think that, when you engage teachers in a conversation,

00:08:58.962 --> 00:09:01.170
you empower them with a curriculum that they actually

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:02.957
know is aligned to the standards.

00:09:02.957 --> 00:09:04.790
They get fired up about the state standards,

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:07.080
in our case, Louisiana students standards.

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:09.750
There may have been, I think, some steps along the way that

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:12.720
didn't give teachers the tools they needed to be confident

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:13.720
when they were doing it.

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:15.690
And when you're not confident, you're fearful.

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:17.190
And when you're fearful, you say it.

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:21.204
And that happened a lot in some states, including our own, occasionally.

00:09:21.204 --> 00:09:22.620
But I think we've bridge that gap.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:23.910
We've gotten beyond it.

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:24.870
And I'll tell you.

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:28.650
I don't feel any more support for high expectations from any group more

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:30.740
than I do from teachers.

00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:32.490
PROFESSOR: What I think I'm hearing, John,

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:35.640
is that you're saying there's been a lot of political conflict

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:38.820
over Common Core State Standards, but this movement

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:43.740
toward setting standards that are closer to being the same across the states

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:47.655
is not going to go away if there's more and deeper support.

00:09:47.655 --> 00:09:50.250
But we've seen this with our Education Next poll

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:53.130
that, in fact, there is a lot of support for similar standards

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:54.690
across the country.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:56.940
And I don't know if that's just what people

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:59.780
say in response to questions that--

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:03.870
but I think I hear you're saying that there is support for that.

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:08.420
JOHN WHITE: I believe that, in a state that has historically been rated low,

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:11.000
that our kids are born just as smart and just as capable

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:12.341
as any kids in this country.

00:10:12.341 --> 00:10:14.090
I believe they have gifts and talents that

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:17.900
are no lesser than those of the children in any other state.

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:21.440
And I believe the people of Louisiana want control over the education system

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:22.010
there.

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:25.190
But they also support the idea that our kids can compete and deserve

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:26.360
a level playing field.

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:30.470
And they shouldn't have to wait until the ACT in 11th grade to get it.

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:33.416
PROFESSOR: Well, we have a new law called the Education--

00:10:33.416 --> 00:10:34.790
oh, I can't remember how it goes.

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:36.414
JOHN WHITE: Every Student Succeeds Act.

00:10:36.414 --> 00:10:38.780
PROFESSOR: Every Student Succeeds Act.

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:42.890
So how is this affecting the whole setting of standards

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:45.014
and the setting of accountability.

00:10:45.014 --> 00:10:46.430
JOHN WHITE: That's a big question.

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:48.110
I mean, I think it's a big if.

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:49.610
States have a lot more control.

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:52.340
Even districts have a lot more control over these questions

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:53.390
than they did under--

00:10:53.390 --> 00:10:55.220
not just No Child Left Behind--

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:57.160
but the waivers to No Child Left Behind.

00:10:57.160 --> 00:11:01.190
And I think whether states make a decision that, on some elements,

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:04.820
they're going to compete, that's still left up in the air.

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:08.330
States also now have the requirement to use measures in their system

00:11:08.330 --> 00:11:12.800
that aren't just about tests and how innovative those things will be,

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:15.680
how much of a free pass those things will be.

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:18.470
I think there's a lot up in the air on those questions.

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:20.900
PROFESSOR: So we don't know exactly where we're

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:25.880
going in our educational system as we enter

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:29.060
the latter part of the second decade of the 21st century.

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:31.700
There's a lot of questions yet to be answered.

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:35.030
But if we don't improve our schools, how are

00:11:35.030 --> 00:11:38.930
we going to be a continually prosperous country

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:40.640
as we move into the 21st century?

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:42.640
JOHN WHITE: It would be impossible for us to be.

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:43.700
And you know, my hope--

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:46.434
and I do believe that in many states this will happen--

00:11:46.434 --> 00:11:48.350
is that we'll go back to the record, and we'll

00:11:48.350 --> 00:11:52.220
see that the following formula is a good formula for success.

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:55.370
One, high expectations that are measured in a way where they

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:57.860
can be validated across state lines.

00:11:57.860 --> 00:12:00.470
Two, an acceptance that our accountability system does

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:01.095
need to evolve.

00:12:01.095 --> 00:12:03.219
There are higher achieving systems around the world

00:12:03.219 --> 00:12:04.670
that have non-testing measures.

00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:06.342
Look at New Zealand, for example.

00:12:06.342 --> 00:12:09.050
And look at what they've done with their system, to great effect.

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:11.420
And three, when schools go off the rails--

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:15.110
and we all in every state have these issues--

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:16.670
you need to be able to intervene.

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:20.910
But we've got a decade of research now to show what works and what doesn't.

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:25.130
So if you are willing to embrace change, willing to embrace high expectations,

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:30.410
and follow the research when there's problems, that's a formula for success.

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:33.920
I don't see why a state would go in a different direction from that.

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:35.405
And if we do, it'll work.

00:12:35.405 --> 00:12:36.530
PROFESSOR: Thank you, John.

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:40.520
This has been a great opportunity to get to understand your philosophy

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:42.399
and your strategy for school reform.

00:12:42.399 --> 00:12:43.440
Thank you for joining us.

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:45.700
JOHN WHITE: Thanks, Paul.

